## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply progressive hydration, per-column lazy-loading, and worker offloading to meet Lighthouse budgets in a heavy dashboard. Also probes SSR/CSR trade-offs and how to validate improvements.

## Key Concepts

- Progressive hydration
- Dynamic imports and code-splitting
- Virtualization
- Web Workers
- Lighthouse budgets and metrics
- IntersectionObserver for loading offscreen content

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example skeleton: dynamic import of heavy cell renderer
import dynamic from 'next/dynamic';
const HeavyCell = dynamic(() => import('./cells/HeavyCell'));

// Progressive hydration with IntersectionObserver
const useColumnHydration = (columnRef) => {
  const [isHydrated, setIsHydrated] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
      ([entry]) => {
        if (entry.isIntersecting) {
          setIsHydrated(true);
          observer.disconnect();
        }
      },
      { threshold: 0.1 }
    );

    if (columnRef.current) {
      observer.observe(columnRef.current);
    }

    return () => observer.disconnect();
  }, []);

  return isHydrated;
};
```

For optimistic updates, you separate the optimistic UI state from the actual server state. Here's a useReducer implementation:

```javascript
const initialState = {
  data: null,
  status: 'idle', // 'idle' | 'pending' | 'success' | 'error'
  previousData: null,
  error: null
};

function reducer(state, action) {
  switch (action.type) {
    case 'OPTIMISTIC_UPDATE':
      return {
        ...state,
        previousData: state.data,
        data: action.payload,
        status: 'pending'
      };
    case 'SUCCESS':
      return {
        ...state,
        status: 'success',
        previousData: null
      };
    case 'ERROR':
      return {
        ...state,
        status: 'error',
        data: state.previousData, // rollback
        previousData: null,
        error: action.error
      };
    default:
      return state;
  }
}

// Usage in component:
const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, initialState);

const handleUpdate = async (newData) => {
  dispatch({ type: 'OPTIMISTIC_UPDATE', payload: newData });
  try {
    await api.update(newData);
    dispatch({ type: 'SUCCESS' });
  } catch (error) {
    dispatch({ type: 'ERROR', error });
  }
};
```

The useState approach uses useEffect for the server call:

```javascript
const [data, setData] = useState(null);
const [isPending, setIsPending] = useState(false);
const [error, setError] = useState(null);

const handleUpdate = async (newData) => {
  const previousData = data;
  setData(newData); // optimistic update
  setIsPending(true);
  try {
    await api.update(newData);
  } catch (error) {
    setData(previousData); // rollback
    setError(error);
  } finally {
    setIsPending(false);
  }
};
```

For race conditions with multiple rapid updates, use AbortController to cancel in-flight requests:

```javascript
const abortRef = useRef(null);

const handleUpdate = async (newData) => {
  // Cancel any in-flight request
  abortRef.current?.abort();

  const controller = new AbortController();
  abortRef.current = controller;

  setData(newData); // optimistic
  try {
    await api.update(newData, { signal: controller.signal });
  } catch (error) {
    if (error.name !== 'AbortError') {
      setData(previousData);
    }
  }
};
```

Alternatively, use version/sequence tracking to ignore stale responses:

```javascript
const versionRef = useRef(0);

const handleUpdate = async (newData) => {
  const currentVersion = ++versionRef.current;
  setData(newData);
  try {
    await api.update(newData);
    if (currentVersion !== versionRef.current) return; // stale response
    // process success
  } catch (error) {
    if (currentVersion !== versionRef.current) return;
    setData(previousData); // rollback
  }
};
```

Key strategies: 1) Store previous state before optimistic update, 2) Use AbortController to cancel stale requests, 3) Track request version to ignore outdated responses, 4) Consider a queue for sequential processing of dependent updates.

## Why This Is Asked

This assesses practical CSS performance and layout stability for dense, dynamic dashboards, focusing on containment, grid behavior, and motion design under continuous data updates.

## Key Concepts

- CSS containment (`contain: layout paint`)
- Grid auto-placement and fixed row heights
- Motion design: transforms vs height changes
- Accessibility: `prefers-reduced-motion`, focus management

## Code Example

```css
